db/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546957 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546957 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546957 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546957 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546957 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546957 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffd11057-b685-55c9-893f-a00c1da187d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546960 #edb/ref 1071 "The data sets $x$ and $y$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=89,\\,{S}_{xy}=-69,\\,{S}_{yy}=426\n$$\n\nCalculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02585424-6726-555e-b4dc-cd93537b87a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546960 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-69)}{\\sqrt{89}\\cdot \\sqrt{426}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{69}{9.433\\,981\\cdot 20.639\\,767} \\\\  & ≈-0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546968 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{2,\\,1,\\,-1\\},\\,y=\\{15,\\,12,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\overset{―}{y}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈1.247,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈2.449.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19d5c66d-e74b-5c3e-98da-49c6a7aecbf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546968 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(2-\\frac{2}{3})(15-12)+(1-\\frac{2}{3})(12-12)+(-1-\\frac{2}{3})(9-12)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{4}{3})(3)+(\\frac{1}{3})(0)+(-\\frac{5}{3})(-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[4+0+5] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{3}{1.247\\cdot 2.449} \\\\  & ≈0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546973 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{23,\\,21,\\,25\\},\\,y=\\{10,\\,8,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=23,$ $\\overset{―}{y}=9,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈1.633,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈0.816.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20c0048f-c0fd-59d4-abe6-fec1d5f41697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546973 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(23-23)(10-9)+(21-23)(8-9)+(25-23)(9-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(0)(1)+(-2)(-1)+(2)(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ≈0.667\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.667}{1.633\\cdot 0.816} \\\\  & ≈0.50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546979 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{7,\\,2,\\,12\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,9,\\,7\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=7,$ $\\overset{―}{y}=5,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈4.082,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈4.320.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24874dea-ae30-58b3-9c5c-bcedba1ee115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546979 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(7-7)(-1-5)+(2-7)(9-5)+(12-7)(7-5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(0)(-6)+(-5)(4)+(5)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[0-20+10] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & ≈-3.333\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(-3.333)}{4.082\\cdot 4.320} \\\\  & ≈-0.19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546986 #edb/ref 1071 "The owner of a fast food stall wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the price per bag of onion rings, in dollars, and\n\n- $y,$ the number of bags of onion rings sold that day.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-5,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=1,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.83$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fb87a4a-c905-590a-aa16-a1b45daeaaf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546986 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-5}{1\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{6} \\\\  & ≈-0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546990 #edb/ref 1071 "A teacher of a group of $4$ students wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the number of hours the students had slept the night before an exam, and $y,$ the student's score in the exam. The data is shown below. \n\n| **Number of hours** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Score ** | $56$ | $68$ | $81$ | $79$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=71.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.81$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "382df20d-c998-524f-8214-a4f7f23f788c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546990 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(2-5)(56-71)+(4-5)(68-71) \\\\  & +(6-5)(81-71)+(8-5)(79-71) \\\\  & =(-3)(-15)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(10)+(3)(8) \\\\  & =45+3+10+24 \\\\  & =82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(2-5)^{2}+(4-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(56-71)^{2}+(68-71)^{2}+(81-71)^{2}+(79-71)^{2} \\\\  & =225+9+100+64 \\\\  & =398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{82}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{398}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{82}{4.472\\,136\\cdot 19.949\\,937} \\\\  & ≈0.92,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546993 #edb/ref 1071 "The data sets $x$ and $y$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=41,\\,{S}_{xy}=-39,\\,{S}_{yy}=61\n$$\n\nCalculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e0b60bf-7e6e-5590-be27-0060180cfb46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546993 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-39)}{\\sqrt{41}\\cdot \\sqrt{61}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{39}{6.403\\,124\\cdot 7.810\\,250} \\\\  & ≈-0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546994 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-8$ and their standard deviations are ${\\sigma }_{x}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=4.$ Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fd5d5a2-1dfb-5532-b6de-c6fae7626334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546994 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-8)}{3\\cdot 4} \\\\  & ≈-0.67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547003 #edb/ref 1071 "Olivia plays soccer. She wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the ages of Olivia's teammates, in years, and\n\n- $y,$ their weights, in kilograms.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=8,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=2,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=5.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho =0.8$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5218551b-c171-5334-a3fb-989e7c3fce63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547003 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{8}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547035 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{5,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,2,\\,5\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "999285af-eeb2-534a-9bcd-a9a462f931b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547035 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(5-5)(-1-2)+(8-5)(2-2)+(2-5)(5-2) \\\\  & =(0)(-3)+(3)(0)+(-3)(3) \\\\  & =0+0-9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(5-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2}+(2-5)^{2} \\\\  & =0+9+9 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+0+9 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-9)}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-9}{3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547041 #edb/ref 1071 "An ergonomist investigates $4$ factories. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the money spent by factory owners on safety training courses, and $y,$ the number of accidents per year in the factories. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Money spent (hundreds of $$$)** | **$10$** | **$15$** | **$12$** | **$23$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of accidents** | $3$ | $6$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=15$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.45$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nHint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aab6da21-f97b-5ce7-b157-a7c2c5069279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547041 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(10-15)(3-4)+(15-15)(6-4) \\\\  & +(12-15)(5-4)+(23-15)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-5)(-1)+(0)(2)+(-3)(1)+(8)(-2) \\\\  & =5+0-3-16 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(10-15)^{2}+(15-15)^{2}+(12-15)^{2}+(23-15)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0+9+64 \\\\  & =98 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-4)^{2}+(6-4)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(2-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{98}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{14}{9.899\\,495\\cdot 3.162\\,278} \\\\  & ≈-0.45,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547043 #edb/ref 1071 "Mark collected information about the weather in his city every day during a particular year. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the temperature, in Celsius degrees, and\n\n- $y,$ the relative humidity, expressed as a percentage.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=75,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=14,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=15.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.44$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1bd60db-c84d-5039-99c1-be6e7b055360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547043 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{75}{14\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{14} \\\\  & ≈0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547068 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547068 #edb/ref 1071 "The data sets $x$ and $y$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=186,\\,{S}_{xy}=2,\\,{S}_{yy}=96\n$$\n\nCalculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e77a74f0-0303-531d-b1fe-933e8eb337ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547068 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{186}\\cdot \\sqrt{96}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2}{13.638\\,182\\cdot 9.797\\,959} \\\\  & ≈0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547078 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{13,\\,4,\\,10\\},\\,y=\\{0,\\,-3,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=9$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f535de3d-b5b2-5621-843d-e4b9ec9ed404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547078 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-9)(0-2)+(4-9)(-3-2)+(10-9)(9-2) \\\\  & =(4)(-2)+(-5)(-5)+(1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+25+7 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-9)^{2}+(4-9)^{2}+(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =16+25+1 \\\\  & =42 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(-3-2)^{2}+(9-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+49 \\\\  & =78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{42}\\cdot \\sqrt{78}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{24}{6.480\\,741\\cdot 8.831\\,761} \\\\  & ≈0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
